proposal.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "bin_center",
      "description": "After a cube was lifted, the bin opening could be obscured in both current views although it had been visible earlier. The policy chose a visibility-recovery lift instead of the recorded closed transfer. Counts and held_color did not preserve the missing spatial fact, breaking SUFFICIENCY. Add bin_center, holding the reliably reconstructed world xy center of the stationary bin's inner opening, or unknown before a reliable reconstruction is available. Set it at the first cycle whose available calibrated views constrain that center; retain it through occlusion and subsequent transfers, releases, and retreats. Revise it only when newly reliable geometry establishes a corrected center or an observed displacement of the bin. Do not fill earlier cycles using geometry available only later in the episode, and do not copy the recorded transfer waypoint as a substitute for visual reconstruction. Example value: [-0.0175, -0.089].",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "bin_center",
      "description": "A lifted cube or the hand can obscure an opening that was previously localizable. Without its retained center, the policy unnecessarily takes a recovery lift, breaking SUFFICIENCY. Initialize bin_center to unknown. When this cycle's calibrated views reliably constrain the inner opening using the action instruction's geometric reconstruction and reprojection checks, write its world xy center, for example [-0.0175, -0.089]. Otherwise retain the incoming value. In particular, do not overwrite a reliable center with an estimate based only on a cropped dark region, an occluding silhouette, or measured TCP position. Update an established value only when reliable current geometry supports a correction or shows that the bin moved. If movement is visibly established but the new center cannot be reconstructed, write unknown. The field records observed bin geometry, never the destination of an action merely being selected."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "When a held cube had reached lift height, requiring fresh bin localization sometimes produced an unnecessary recovery lift; other transfers used inaccurate estimates from partial rim views. The former is a SUFFICIENCY failure because earlier observable geometry was lost. Replace the unconditional requirement to re-estimate the bin center each cycle with this rule: use a reliable current reconstruction when available; otherwise use incoming bin_center unless current observations establish that the bin moved. Use that same center for closed transfer at z=0.20, the separate centered opening operation, and retreat at [bin_x-0.10, bin_y, 0.20]. A partial or cropped current view does not invalidate a retained center. Perform visibility recovery only when neither current geometry nor retained memory supplies a reliable center. Keep the existing inner-rim reconstruction and reprojection checks; do not replace them with episode-specific coordinates. At release, correct both lateral coordinates to the opening center before checking the nonzero-motion requirement; a sufficient lateral correction makes an added vertical displacement unnecessary. All deposit-count, held-color, grasp, final-retreat, and button-contact rules remain unchanged.",
  "refused": "The reports of ambiguous cube selection correctly identify a SUFFICIENCY gap: eligibility and clear-grasp preferences do not uniquely predict which accessible cube the recording selects. However, their proposed scene-specific choices do not establish a common executable ranking. Nearest-to-hand, robot-rightmost, and isolated-foreground preferences are not interchangeable, and imposing any one globally would contradict other recorded selections. One report also describes the next selected object as a foreground green cube while assigning it the coordinates of a distant blue cube; the subsequent grasp and blue deposit confirm that the selected cube was blue. Do not add that color claim, a per-scene choice table, a prescribed color order, or a future-target memory field. The target-selection gap remains unresolved by this minibatch; resolving it requires an independently supported selection criterion, not pretending that arbitrary recorded choices are recoverable historical facts. Reports of inaccurate bin reconstruction are addressed only by retaining genuinely observed geometry where it later becomes unavailable. Their numerical targets are not general rules and must not be hard-coded. Reports that release should correct the measured TCP to the opening center repeat an existing rule, retained explicitly above rather than introducing another stage or memory flag. The incorrect descent onto a neighboring cube and the small cube/button localization errors likewise do not demonstrate missing history: current calibrated views provide the relevant centers, and existing instructions already require maintaining the aligned cube, using its top face, and localizing the circular button top. No new memory is justified for those errors, and no deposit-count or held_color annotation change is supported."
}

optimizer.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "kind": "add",
      "field": "bin_center",
      "description": "World xy center of the bin's inner opening, written as [x, y] in metres, or unknown. Initially unknown. Set at the first cycle whose available calibrated views reliably constrain the center using geometric reconstruction and reprojection checks. Retain through occlusion, transfers, releases, and retreats. Revise only when reliable current geometry establishes a correction or an observed displacement of the bin. If displacement is established but the new center cannot be reconstructed, write unknown. Use only geometry available by this cycle; do not backfill from later views or substitute recorded action destinations for visual reconstruction. e.g. [-0.02, -0.09]",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "kind": "add",
      "field": "bin_center",
      "description": "World xy center of the bin's inner opening, written as [x, y] in metres, or unknown. Initialize to unknown. When this cycle's calibrated views reliably constrain the center using the action instruction's geometric reconstruction and reprojection checks, write that center. Otherwise retain the incoming value through occlusion, transfers, releases, and retreats. Update an established value only when reliable current geometry supports a correction or establishes bin displacement. If displacement is established but the new center cannot be reconstructed, write unknown. A cropped view or partial occlusion alone does not invalidate the retained center. Do not overwrite it with the centroid of a visible dark fragment, an occluding silhouette, measured TCP position, or the destination of an action being selected. e.g. [-0.02, -0.09]"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": [
    {
      "modify_from": "Reconstruct the bin's rectangular inner opening using the available calibrated current views.",
      "modify_to": "For the bin center, use a reliable current reconstruction when available; otherwise use incoming bin_center unless current observations establish that the bin moved. Partial occlusion or cropping alone does not invalidate a retained center. To obtain or reliably correct the center, reconstruct the bin's rectangular inner opening using the available calibrated current views."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Use the purposeful visibility-recovery motion below only when the available geometry is genuinely underconstrained; do not invent a numeric rim height. Use this same geometrically defined center for closed transfer and separate release at z=0.20 and for retreat at [bin_x-0.10,bin_y,0.20].",
      "modify_to": "Use the purposeful visibility-recovery motion below only when neither current geometry nor a valid retained bin_center supplies a reliable center; do not invent a numeric rim height. Use the center selected by this current-reconstruction-or-memory rule consistently for closed transfer and separate release at z=0.20 and for retreat at [bin_x-0.10,bin_y,0.20]."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Re-estimate positions from current views; scene objects can appear during execution.",
      "modify_to": "Re-estimate cube and button positions from current views; scene objects can appear during execution. For the bin, apply the current-reconstruction-or-memory rule rather than requiring fresh localization."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Once lifted, move to the visually localized world xy center of the bin opening at z=0.20, keeping the cube closed in the gripper and preserving the measured grasp orientation. After the closed-hand transfer reaches approximately z=0.20 above the bin, re-localize the opening and command open at [bin_x, bin_y, 0.20], correcting any residual lateral error in that waypoint.",
      "modify_to": "Once lifted, move to the world xy center of the bin opening supplied by the current-reconstruction-or-memory rule at z=0.20, keeping the cube closed in the gripper and preserving the measured grasp orientation. After the closed-hand transfer reaches approximately z=0.20 above the bin, use that same rule to obtain the opening center and command open at [bin_x, bin_y, 0.20], correcting any residual lateral error in that waypoint."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Compute this offset from the visually localized bin center, never from measured TCP xy.",
      "modify_to": "Compute this offset from the bin center supplied by the current-reconstruction-or-memory rule, never from measured TCP xy."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "If occlusion leaves an object needed for localization genuinely underconstrained after using the available calibrated views and visible geometry, move upward to a clear viewing height while retaining any held cube, then re-localize. Do not make this recovery motion merely because the wrist view crops the object or part of it is hidden.",
      "modify_to": "If occlusion leaves an object needed for localization genuinely underconstrained after using the available calibrated views and visible geometry, and for the bin no valid retained bin_center supplies its center, move upward to a clear viewing height while retaining any held cube, then re-localize. Do not make this recovery motion merely because the wrist view crops the object or part of it is hidden."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "The earlier rejection of remembered bin coordinates is superseded only because this round establishes that previously observable bin geometry can become unavailable in both current views. The new field preserves that observed fact; it does not replace calibrated reconstruction or authorize copying recorded waypoints. No scene-specific coordinates, fixed rim height, target-choice table, color priority, nearest-cube rule, or future-target memory is added. Across rounds these selection proposals remain inconsistent and do not establish a predictable general ranking. No duplicate release-correction stage, yaw history, cube/button position memory, or changes to deposit counts and held_color are committed: the existing rules cover those operations, and this round establishes no additional missing historical fact."
}
